Q5. DUAL-DATABASE CONSISTENCY (MYSQL VS. SQLITE)
================================================================================
Question:
In your architecture, you commit business state to MySQL and then write an audit log to SQLite (audit_logs.db). What happens if MySQL commits successfully, but the server crashes before SQLite finishes writing? Also, why would SQLite fail in a multi-container deployment?

Model Answer:
1. Failure Mode & Outbox Pattern: Because MySQL and SQLite are separate engines without a distributed two-phase commit, a crash between commits can cause missing audit logs. At scale, use the Transactional Outbox Pattern—writing the audit event into an outbox table inside the same MySQL transaction, and having a background worker asynchronously flush events to the audit store.
2. Container Scaling Limitation: SQLite is file-based and locks the entire file during writes. If we scale zaika_backend horizontally across 3 Docker containers behind a load balancer, each container would have its own isolated local .db file (causing split audit logs) or suffer "database is locked" errors on a shared network volume. For multi-instance production, migrate audit logs to PostgreSQL/MySQL or Elasticsearch.

